meta {
  name: login
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{vipa}}/api/v1/auth/login
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{csrfToken}}
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "admin@admin.com",
    "password": "{{adminPassword}}"
  }
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

docs {
  Run `csrf` before this one. Since US-006 the login is a write like any other and is
  refused with 403 without a token — which is the point: it means nobody can be signed
  in from another site either. Signing in then swaps the token for a fresh one; the
  collection-level post-response script picks the new one up, so the sequence keeps
  working without a second trip to `csrf`.
  
  Answers 200 with the signed-in user (id, first name, last name, email, role) and
  sets the JSESSIONID cookie. Bruno keeps that cookie, so login -> me -> logout -> me
  runs as a sequence.
  
  Nothing in this folder works until the account has a local identity, and the dev
  database has none: auth_identity is empty and seed.sql inserts nothing into it.
  Seeding is US-007. Until then, create one by hand:
  
    htpasswd -bnBC 10 "" 'test-password' | tr -d ':\n'
  
    INSERT INTO auth_identity (user_id, provider, password_hash)
    VALUES ((SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = 'alice.martin@example.com'), 'local', '<hash>');
  
  Do not put that hash in seed.sql — a known password committed to the repository
  outlives the phase it was meant for.
  
  Every failure answers the same 401 body, whether the email is unknown, the password
  wrong, the account inactive, or the local identity missing. That is deliberate: a
  distinct message would turn this endpoint into a way to enumerate the school's
  addresses. The cause is in the server log.
}
